Differences between the initial UML and the Recent one:
- AvatarState (ENUM)
When implementing the Avatar class it became obvious that
it is important to track the previous and current states of the avatar. 
(For example: Is the avatar in flight and we should decrease its energy?
 Was the avatar idle and now we need to add to its velocity?) 
The simplest way to solve it was using and ENUM.
- ImageManager (Class)
As the name implies, a class for managing the reading of the images.
 At first we assumed each class will read and associate the images it needs with a specific reader,
However, after we started implementing we saw the programm will be more clear and readable if we created a
dedicated class that will take care of the process of reading the images from the memory.
- CreateAnimations (Method in Avatar)
We needed a method that creates and saves animation renderables corresponding  to the avatar movements.
Only upon implementing it we realised it needed to be public. Only the static method "createAvatar" 
receives and ImageReader that is used to create renderables. 
It forced the CreateAnimations to be public and static as well.
- Bonuses: 
Didn't plan them ahead of time because we weren't sure if 
we will implement them.
- FloatCounter - A counter for the avatar energy
- NumericEnergyCounter - Numerically represents the energy of the avatar.
- MemoryGameManager+MemoryGameCard - Manages the memory of the games
- Background music


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Making the world endless:
The main idea dividing the game into chunks. 
In each moment there will be up to two chunks that the game has to keep track of.
(Where trees, terrain and the avatar will be). We created three arrays, "main" "right", "left"
that keep track of the ranges of the chunks.
In each moment the avatar is in the main chunk. When he
comes close* to the left (or right) edge, we create the  
left (or right) chunk.
Each time the avatar migrates to the left (or right)
chunk, we delete the right (or left) chunk, and renaming the current chunk to be the main one.
We wanted to keep the world the same, so that when the avatar went far away from a certain spot and
 then later returned to it, the terrain and the trees will be the same.
So for each game run we created a seed. The metods that create the terrain and the trees,
 uses the seed and the current coordinates when deciding on the height of the ground and the trees.
 That way the game will look the same when revisited.


* we set a fixed margin for each chunk, so that the path (the next chunk) of the avatar 
will not be created the moment he crosses to the next chunk, but earlier.
This creates a smoother game play.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Creating the trees package:
Consists of two classes Trees, and Leaf.
Trees:
The mainresponsibility of the class is to create trees in a certain range.
The main two functions of this class are:
1. static public method createInRange that creates trees in a given range. 
2. a private function createTree that creates a singles tree 
(and is called by the function createInRange)
This function creates the trunk of the tree and its leaves and add them to the game.

We decided not to create a dedicated class to the trunk because it does not gives us any advantage,
 as the trunk as a whole doesn't have a special functionality.
That is why in the function that creates a single tree after getting a random tree height,
we created the trunk as a seperate blocks stacked on each other and then added the leaves
 according to the trunk's placement.
To create the leaves of the tree we called the static method createFoliage in the class Leaf. 
Basically, the class Trees contains different objects that are necessary to create a tree,
 but its as a whole does not have any behaviour.
The trunk acts like a trunk, the trees as leaves, and the and the object of type Tress 
acts as a factory
 for creating those objects.

Leaf:
The main functions of this class:
1. Static public method createFoliage that created foliage according to the coordinates and size
 it receives as an input.
2. fadeOut, death and life functions that are responsible for the life cycle of the leaf.
3. Functions that are connected to the bonus memory game (we will elaborate about them in the bonus
 section down bellow)

Unlike other blocks we used in the game, like terrain and trunk blocks, a leaf needs to fall of,
and change its behaviour according to its life cycle. In other words - he needs a complicated 
functionality that is unique 
to it, so we saw the need to create a seperate class that represents leaves, following the principle of encapsulation.
We decided to create a class for a single leave and not for a group of them,
 because each leave has field like life duration and screen placement, that is different from leaf to leaf. 
Again, as the case with the trunk, the group of the leaves has no speacial charachteristic, 
there is not difference between on or other group of leaves.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Desgin Decisions:
- Energy display
We wanted to display the energy of the avatar on the screen.
Like we did in previous excersices in the course, we we wanted to create a Counter object and display it
using a graphical object.
the problem we ran into was that we wanted to decrease the enrgy by 0.5 in each frame, but the default
counter in danogl library is an integer counter.
We considered 3 solutions:
* Using danogl.Counter and also a float member which will hold the energy, update them both together
  and display the float energy value.
* Using danogl.Counter and count from 0 to 200,  each frame decrease the value by 1, and when displaying
  the value divided by 2.
* Creating a custom Counter class which resembles in structure the danogl.Counter, BUT counts in floats, and
  round the value when displaying it.

We chose the third option.
The first option is a redundent duplication of code, the second option can cause problems if we would like to
change the maximal energy bound
or the amount of energy being decreased in each frame, and might get into complicated calculations when
wanting to display this float as int.

- Ground height
Some of the classes, like Tree and Leaf, has to get information from the ground they are on, like the ground
height, for their creation and update.
At first, we thought to just send each of these classes the Terrain object created for the game, so they'll
have access to everything they need.
After some more thought we realized this solution will create a non-necessary dependency, because all these
classes really need is the groundHeightAt
function. So we solved the problem using a functional interface: we sent the classes constructos the height
function of terrain as argument (Function<Float, Float> groundHeightAt ).

- Reading Images
At first we assumed each class will read the images it needs and load them itself, but after we started
implementing we saw that writing the paths of the images hardcoded in the code looks long and busy and makes
you scroll a lot to get to the game logic, especially when creating animations.
In addition, the loading image logic was similar between classes, so in order to get rid of code duplications
and create more readable code, we decided to create a designated class - ImagesManager, which will hold
all the images paths needed for the game and will handle reading the images from the disc.

- Creating foliage
At first, the function which creates foliage was located inside the Tree class, but we realized that creating
a group of leaves together is logically more suitable in the Leaf class,
just like creating a group of terrain blocks is a function of the Terrain class and creating a group of trees
id a fuction of the Tree class.
That way it also matches the appearance of leaves in the real world - groups of leaves don't appear only on
trees, but on bushes as well, so it is more reasonable for this function to be part of the Leaf class.

